  "image_name": "1e85ce246c5cb2c1e4fee7522a7e0651.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how Notch signaling appears to regulate the balance between proliferation, adhesion, and differentiation in a stratified epithelium. The tissue is drawn as an immature basal layer of stem or progenitor cells attached to a basement membrane, with progressively more differentiated cells stacked above and flattened terminally differentiated cells at the surface.\n\nNotch is shown as a central control node with several downstream effects. It promotes differentiation through factors such as IRF6, retinoic acid signaling, and HES/HEY, driving basal progenitors upward into differentiating layers. At the same time, Notch induces p21 and interacts with p63, leading to inhibition of proliferation in the basal compartment. Notch also seems to restrain p63 more directly, reinforcing the reduction in cell division.\n\nA second branch links basal cells to the basement membrane through ROCK1/ROCK2 and MRCKα, indicating a role in adhesion and tissue architecture. Upstream, AP1 and p53 are placed near Notch with inhibitory connections, although one link is marked with a question mark, suggesting some uncertainty.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that Notch functions mainly as a pro-differentiation, anti-proliferative regulator that helps maintain orderly epithelial layering.",
